{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "professional_principle": "Atomicity groups the enrollment write and seat-count update as one all-or-nothing unit. If either fails, rollback undoes all changes in that transaction. Atomicity is distinct from isolation and capacity-race protection.", "valid_solutions": ["A transaction encompassing both changes, committing together on success and rolling back both on any failure.", "An equivalent conceptual all-or-nothing operation with explicit failure reversal."], "scope": "Credit explicit transaction grouping plus rollback without requiring the word atomicity or an isolation-level name. Evaluate cache safety and concurrent capacity reservation in their own criteria; neither substitutes for nor negates an otherwise correct description of rollback."}, "submission": "Author: SyntheticAuthorMaple\nI start a transaction and hold an exclusive lock on the shared course-capacity row. After acquiring that lock I read the current capacity again; when there is a seat I decrement it and insert the enrollment, then commit and release the lock. The next request waits for the lock and rechecks the updated availability. Failure of either write rolls back all transaction changes. Separately, a UNIQUE key on (student, course) rejects repeated enrollment by the same student; this is different from the lock protecting capacity across students. Cached course availability makes browsing faster but can be stale even with a short TTL. Enrollment uses the database recheck under the lock, never cached availability."}
